[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its own table shows two causes, and
footnotes a third thing. Read by tenant, not by subject line, the ticket holds **three problems**.
Each one now has a ticket of its own in the desk's new tracker, with the reports it covers.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed and the booking-v3 form is retired.
The one-day move was not part of that work. Its ticket stays open, and its reports have nowhere
to go now that #4471 is closed.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<TicketSplit ticket={4471} />

## What the desk did

Engineering's update on #4471, 5 October:

| problem | engineering said | what it means here |
|---|---|---|
| parser | **fixed** · `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` | the fix the desk queued; its ticket is closed as done |
| hint | **won't fix** · booking-v3 form retired | the form with the wrong hint is gone; its ticket is closed, not planned |
| one-day-move | nothing | not a parser fix, not a form; still open |

A fix for the parser and a retired form do not move a date by one day. If the three tenants
that ran booking-v3 move to a form with the same hint, the hint problem comes back under a new
name. Check that before calling it over.

## The three problems

**parser.** Every tenant on file writes the day first, and the shared parser reads every date
month first. `7 April` becomes 4 July. It reaches people through the app, by email and through
the booking-v2 form, which asks for `DD/MM/YYYY` and passes it straight to the parser. The
queued fix is for this one.

**hint.** The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the
hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The result looks
the same as the parser bug, a flipped month, but only booking-v3 tenants hit it, only through the
form, and fixing the parser will not fix it. The hint and the form have to agree.

**one-day-move.** Five reports are not a flipped month. The date moves by one day: a Tuesday that
shows as Monday, every booking in one month one day off, one meeting that reads Tuesday in
Lisbon and Monday in Toronto, an invite that arrived before the booking was made. That is a day
boundary crossed, most likely a time zone or UTC conversion, and it is a different bug again. The
desk read these as a one-day move when they came in and noted it, but the subject rule filed
them under the parser.

## How the split was made

- One id, one report. The export pages overlap, so the same report can appear twice.
- The problem is the tenant's: their locale and which booking form they run, from the tenant
  directory. Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser and is wrong often enough that
  nothing here counts it.
- A report the desk read as a one-day move belongs to that problem, not to the tenant's cause.

The table above runs this on every build. If the desk merges more reports into #4471, the counts
move with it.

## What to do

1. Investigate the one-day move. Start with R-41344: one meeting, two cities, two days. R-41620
   came in the day before #4471 was closed, and its reporter says people have stopped reporting.
2. Check the form the booking-v3 tenants (alder.physio, copper.studio, pale.harbour) moved to for
   the same `MM/DD/YYYY` hint.
3. Watch for flipped months after the parser fix. One report from any channel is a regression
   and a new ticket, not a reopened #4471.
4. Stop merging on subject alone. [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) was
   written for speed during an incident, and it is how three problems became one ticket. Merge
   on cause, with the tenant in hand.

